      "lot_url": "https://www.sixbid.com/en/heritage-auctions-inc/13941/ancients-roman-imperial/12356002/licinia-eudoxia-western-roman-empire",
      "title": "Licinia Eudoxia, Western Roman Empire (ca. AD 439-490). AV tremissis (14mm, 1.48 gm, 6h). NGC MS 5/5 - 3/5, wavy flan, brushed. Constantinople, AD 444. AEL EVDO-XIA AVG, pearl-diademed, draped bust of Licinia Eudoxia right, seen from front, wearing necklace and earring, hair elaborately weaved with long plait up the back of head and tucked under diadem / Cross within wreath terminating in large jewel; wreath type 11, CONOB star below. RIC X (Theodosius II) 336 or 346.  Although the daughter of Eastern Roman Emperor Theodosius II, Licinia Eudoxia would marry into the Western Roman Empire. At only two years old, she would be betrothed to her first cousin, once removed: four-year-old Valentinian III. When they came of age, they would marry - marking the reunion of the House of Theodosius. However, in AD 455, Valentinian III would be assassinated and Eudoxia would be forced to marry new Emperor Petronius Maximus only days later. Enraged with Maximus' tyranny, she would summon the Vandal King Gaiseric who would capture Rome and defeat Maximus. When Gaiseric returned to Carthage, he would take Eudoxia, and her two daughters, who supposedly lived there with honor. She would return with one daughter to Constantinople twenty-five years later by ransom of Leo I.   HID05401242017  \u00a9 2026 Heritage Auctions | All Rights Reserved www.HA.com/TexasAuctioneerLicenseNotice",
